<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea · religion</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ash'arism</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Ash'ari theology</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Ash'arism is a school of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sunni-islam">Sunni</a> Islamic theology founded by Abu al-Hasan al-Ash'ari (874–936) in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iraq">Iraq</a>. It used rational argument to defend traditional doctrines such as God's omnipotence and the uncreated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quran">Quran</a>, taking a middle path between the rationalist Mu'tazilites and literalist traditionists. Promoted in the Seljuk <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madrasa">madrasas</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nizam-al-mulk">Nizam al-Mulk</a> and by thinkers such as al-Ghazali, it became the dominant theology of Sunni <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a>.</p></div>
